WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.098
Good morning Ziad.

00:00:01.098 --> 00:00:03.049
It's Monday, October nineteenth.

00:00:03.049 --> 00:00:06.159
The weather today is about as good as October gets.

00:00:06.159 --> 00:00:11.951
It's sunny all day in Bushwick, with a high around sixty-four and a low tonight near fifty-one.

00:00:11.951 --> 00:00:14.146
There's basically no chance of rain.

00:00:14.146 --> 00:00:21.829
The wind is light out of the north-northeast this afternoon, then picks up a bit tonight, around ten to fifteen miles an hour.

00:00:21.829 --> 00:00:25.122
A light jacket or a flannel over a T-shirt covers you.

00:00:25.122 --> 00:00:29.207
You'll want the extra layer by the time you head home this evening.

00:00:29.207 --> 00:00:30.549
Your day starts early.

00:00:30.549 --> 00:00:34.695
You've got the gym at seven, then a full work day from nine to five.

00:00:34.695 --> 00:00:43.720
After that, there's an appointment at five forty-five, and it's a video call, so plan to be somewhere quiet with a good connection right after work.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.890
That's it for the calendar, so the evening is yours.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.939
Here are three stories worth knowing this morning.

00:00:49.939 --> 00:00:50.793
First, France.

00:00:50.793 --> 00:00:57.134
Protests over the cost of living, joined by students, turned violent in several cities over the weekend.

00:00:57.134 --> 00:01:01.585
In Limoges, a historic town hall was set on fire and its spire collapsed.

00:01:01.585 --> 00:01:05.549
There was also looting and clashes in Paris, Lyon, and Marseille.

00:01:05.549 --> 00:01:11.890
President Macron has admitted there were intelligence failures, and the unrest flared up again in Paris.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:17.378
It's worth watching, because similar youth-led protests have been spreading across Europe.

00:01:17.378 --> 00:01:20.183
Second, the weather itself is becoming an A.I.

00:01:20.183 --> 00:01:20.549
story.

00:01:20.549 --> 00:01:27.256
Forecasters are watching what could be a record El Niño, possibly the strongest since at least nineteen-fifty.

00:01:27.256 --> 00:01:29.390
That's a real test for the new A.I.

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:30.305
weather models.

00:01:30.305 --> 00:01:39.085
They learned from the last few decades of data, and some scientists worry they won't handle conditions far outside anything they've seen before.

00:01:39.085 --> 00:01:47.805
On a brighter note, Amazon deforestation in Brazil just hit a record low, though El Niño's drought risk is one of the threats to that progress.

00:01:47.805 --> 00:01:51.159
Third, OpenAI confirmed it's building a humanoid robot.

00:01:51.159 --> 00:02:01.463
Sam Altman says to expect a demo sometime in twenty twenty-seven, though he admitted robots that are actually ready for the street are a few years further out than that.

00:02:01.463 --> 00:02:05.549
Meanwhile, some big banks say they're already blocking outside A.I.

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:10.671
agents that try to log in to customer accounts, sometimes dozens of attempts a week.

00:02:10.671 --> 00:02:15.061
So the agents are arriving, and the walls are going up at the same time.

00:02:15.061 --> 00:02:18.902
There's a lot of good free music in the neighborhood this week.

00:02:18.902 --> 00:02:25.305
On Wednesday at seven, Sleepwalk is hosting Sonosphere, an ambient and experimental night, and it's free.

00:02:25.305 --> 00:02:32.805
If you'd rather move, Saturday night at ten-thirty Sleepwalk turns into Cult Disco, which is disco line dancing, also free.

00:02:32.805 --> 00:02:39.024
And if you have energy left after your appointment tonight, there's a free show at Sleepwalk at seven.

00:02:39.024 --> 00:02:41.707
Listings can change, so check before you go.

00:02:41.707 --> 00:02:44.451
Today's challenge is a two-minute voice note.

00:02:44.451 --> 00:02:48.963
Think of one person you like but haven't really talked to in a few months.

00:02:48.963 --> 00:02:51.707
Not a big catch-up, no planning, no pressure.

00:02:51.707 --> 00:02:58.476
Just send them a voice message, under two minutes, telling them one specific thing that made you think of them.

00:02:58.476 --> 00:03:01.098
A song, a street, a joke, a photo you took.

00:03:01.098 --> 00:03:05.061
Voice beats text here because they get to hear that you meant it.

00:03:05.061 --> 00:03:10.793
Do it on your walk to work or between sets at the gym, before you can talk yourself out of it.

00:03:10.793 --> 00:03:12.378
Here's where the word O.K.

00:03:12.378 --> 00:03:12.988
came from.

00:03:12.988 --> 00:03:16.951
In the late eighteen-thirties, Boston newspapers had a goofy fad.

00:03:16.951 --> 00:03:21.280
Writers would abbreviate phrases as if they were misspelled on purpose.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:26.829
So "oll wright" became O.W., and "oll korrect," a joke spelling of all correct, became O.K.

00:03:26.829 --> 00:03:31.098
In March of eighteen thirty-nine, the Boston Morning Post printed O.K.

00:03:31.098 --> 00:03:34.573
in exactly that sense, and that's the earliest known use.

00:03:34.573 --> 00:03:36.829
Most of those jokes died out quickly.

00:03:36.829 --> 00:03:37.073
O.K.

00:03:37.073 --> 00:03:39.756
survived because of a presidential campaign.

00:03:39.756 --> 00:03:48.293
In eighteen-forty, Martin Van Buren was running for reelection, and his nickname was Old Kinderhook, after his hometown in upstate New York.

00:03:48.293 --> 00:03:49.878
His supporters formed O.K.

00:03:49.878 --> 00:03:52.805
Clubs, and the letters were suddenly everywhere.

00:03:52.805 --> 00:03:57.927
The joke spelling and the campaign slogan reinforced each other, and the word stuck.

00:03:57.927 --> 00:04:01.890
Today it's one of the most widely understood words on the planet.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:05.122
And it started as a newspaper gag about bad spelling.

00:04:05.122 --> 00:04:06.341
That's your morning.

00:04:06.341 --> 00:04:10.000
Have a strong lift, a good day at work, and an easy evening.
